Right — that's consistent with what I said: only 10/23 gets written, because the save is happening once, after the loop, on the last iteration's values. The other six days parse in memory and then get thrown away before Save-WeeklyWorksheet ever sees them.

The fix is the two brace edits. To be explicit about what's wrong in your file right now:

```powershell
                Rows      = $decorated
		}}          ← this second } is closing the foreach early
```

and the actual foreach-close is missing at the bottom — it should be sitting on its own line right after:

```powershell
            $saved += [PSCustomObject]@{ Date = $dt.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd"); Rows = $decorated.Count }
        }          ← add this
    } finally {
```

Either make those two edits, or replace the block from $saved = @() through the closing } finally { with the version I pasted in my last message. Then:

1. Save the file
2. Close and relaunch the app (PowerShell caches function bodies — editing the file alone won't fix the running process)
3. Re-fetch or re-paste the eDAC HTML
4. Check the log — the last line should read imported 7 date(s), ~91 total row(s) instead of 13
5. Check Work Tracking\Weekly Worksheets\ — you should have 7 JSONs, not 1

Nothing you have to delete first; Save-WeeklyWorksheet overwrites by date, and Import-WorksheetJobsToJobLog dedupes on (WO#, date), so re-importing is safe.